From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:46:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] replay: the replay mutex pairs on its holder, not on
 replay_mode

replay_mutex_lock() and replay_mutex_unlock() are no-ops while
replay_mode is NONE and assume the mode never changes in between.
x-rr-end-record (0040) breaks that: it sets the mode to NONE in a QMP
command, i.e. while the main loop holds the mutex. The main loop's
unlock before its next poll is then a no-op, the thread-local flag stays
set and the ticket counters stay one apart. The next x-rr-begin-record
calls replay_mutex_init() again, which re-initializes the mutex and
takes one more ticket, so mutex_tail runs two ahead of mutex_head: the
first replay_mutex_lock() after that begin waits for ever.

Unlock now releases whatever this thread holds, in any mode (and still
asserts if it holds nothing while record/replay is on).
replay_mutex_init() initializes the mutex and condition once, and gives
the caller the mutex only if it does not hold it already (an end and a
begin in the same main-loop iteration); it asserts that nobody else does.
---
 replay/replay-internal.c | 48 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 41 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/replay/replay-internal.c b/replay/replay-internal.c
index 4839f2b63213898461922c9d5e6fcd3c7704c2f7..7c60720cce6606585871c9dcf075c0fc1b0b5c0f 100644
--- a/replay/replay-internal.c
+++ b/replay/replay-internal.c
@@ -231,15 +231,42 @@ void replay_finish_event(void)
     replay_fetch_data_kind();
 }
 
+/*
+ * Whether *this thread* holds the replay mutex.  Lock and unlock pair on this,
+ * not on replay_mode: x-rr-begin-record and x-rr-end-record switch the mode
+ * at run time, and a thread whose lock ran in one mode may unlock in another.
+ */
 static __thread bool replay_locked;
 
 void replay_mutex_init(void)
 {
-    qemu_mutex_init(&lock);
-    qemu_cond_init(&mutex_cond);
-    /* Hold the mutex while we start-up */
-    replay_locked = true;
-    ++mutex_tail;
+    static bool initialized;
+
+    /*
+     * Called each time record/replay starts: at startup, and again by every
+     * x-rr-begin-record / x-rr-begin-replay.  Initialize once; re-initializing
+     * would reset a mutex that a previous recording's threads used.
+     */
+    if (!initialized) {
+        qemu_mutex_init(&lock);
+        qemu_cond_init(&mutex_cond);
+        initialized = true;
+    }
+    /*
+     * Hold the mutex while we start up: the caller (vl.c, or the main loop
+     * running a QMP command) holds the BQL and leaves through a
+     * replay_mutex_unlock().  It may still hold the mutex from a recording
+     * that ended in this same main-loop iteration; then it keeps it.
+     * Otherwise nobody holds it: with replay_mode == NONE, lock is a no-op,
+     * and unlock below releases a mutex held across the end of a recording.
+     */
+    if (!replay_locked) {
+        qemu_mutex_lock(&lock);
+        g_assert(mutex_head == mutex_tail);
+        ++mutex_tail;
+        replay_locked = true;
+        qemu_mutex_unlock(&lock);
+    }
 }
 
 bool replay_mutex_locked(void)
@@ -266,13 +293,20 @@ void replay_mutex_lock(void)
 
 void replay_mutex_unlock(void)
 {
-    if (replay_mode != REPLAY_MODE_NONE) {
-        g_assert(replay_mutex_locked());
+    /*
+     * Release what this thread took, whatever the mode is now: if the
+     * recording ended while we held the mutex, it is still ours to give back
+     * (else the next recording's first lock would wait for ever).  Unlocking
+     * a mutex we don't hold is a bug while record/replay is on.
+     */
+    if (replay_locked) {
         qemu_mutex_lock(&lock);
         ++mutex_head;
         replay_locked = false;
         qemu_cond_broadcast(&mutex_cond);
         qemu_mutex_unlock(&lock);
+    } else {
+        g_assert(replay_mode == REPLAY_MODE_NONE);
     }
 }
 
